FreeType
--------
crates/ferrokit-gui/src/text/ftgrays.rs is a Rust port of parts of the FreeType 2.14.2 font engine
(https://freetype.org): the anti-aliasing rasterizer src/smooth/ftgrays.c and src/smooth/ftsmooth.c,
and the bitmap box and outline walk of src/base/ftobjs.c and src/base/ftoutln.c. Portions of this
software are copyright (C) 1996-2025 The FreeType Project (www.freetype.org). All rights reserved.
That code is used under the FreeType License (LICENSES/FTL.txt):

  Copyright (C) 1996-2025 by
  David Turner, Robert Wilhelm, and Werner Lemberg.

FreeType is offered under two mutually exclusive licences, of which one must be chosen (its
LICENSE.TXT): the FreeType License (FTL) or the GNU GPL version 2 or later. ferrokit uses the FTL,
which is compatible with the GPL version 3 that the LGPL-3.0-only is built on; the GPL alternative
would put the file under a stronger copyleft than the rest of the library. The file therefore
carries `LGPL-3.0-only AND FTL`: the FreeType-derived code under the FTL, ferrokit's own changes
under the project licence. The FTL's credit and no-warranty terms apply to every binary that
contains it (see above). The FTL text in LICENSES/FTL.txt is the file docs/FTL.TXT of FreeType
2.14.2, unchanged.


Vendored crates (third_party/)
------------------------------
third_party/skrifa (skrifa 0.49.0) and third_party/read-fonts (read-fonts 0.46.0) are copies of
the crates.io releases of the fontations project (https://github.com/googlefonts/fontations,
commit 47a301615fd95acf0da9fb7dbcd0ea7c7df36943), Copyright (c) 2019 Fontations Developers,
licensed under MIT OR Apache-2.0; their LICENSE-MIT and LICENSE-APACHE are in each directory.
ferrokit uses them under the MIT license. Each directory's README.ferrokit.md lists the changes
made to the upstream code (among them a port of parts of FreeType 2.14.2's TrueType interpreter
and CFF engine, under the FreeType License above, as the Rust port of ftgrays is).

Crates added to Cargo.lock with them (all from crates.io, unmodified): font-types (MIT OR
Apache-2.0), once_cell (MIT OR Apache-2.0), stable_deref_trait (MIT OR Apache-2.0), foldhash
(Zlib), bytemuck_derive (Zlib OR Apache-2.0 OR MIT), synstructure (MIT), yoke, yoke-derive,
zerofrom and zerofrom-derive (Unicode-3.0).


Easing equations
----------------
Qt's QEasingCurve uses Robert Penner's Easing Equations, which qtbase ships under the BSD 3-Clause
license (src/3rdparty/easing/easing.cpp). The curves of ferrokit-core (animation/easing.rs: elastic,
back, bounce and the others, with the default period 0.3 and overshoot 1.70158) are the same
equations, so that notice applies to them too (LICENSES/BSD-3-Clause.txt):

  Copyright (c) 2001 Robert Penner
  All rights reserved.


Reference data
--------------
Expected values in many tests (for example crates/ferrokit-widgets/tests/fixtures/layout_qt_oracle.txt,
and the numbers in the text, layout and style sheet tests) were measured by running Qt through
PySide6 6.11.2 (Qt 6.11.2). The file fixtures/layout_qt_oracle_known.txt next to it lists the cases
where ferrokit differs from Qt; they were recorded the same way.


